1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:05,105
Our UROP project studies multimodal fake news classification.

2
00:00:05,225 --> 00:00:12,044
Multimodal simply means using more than one kind of input: here, a short post title and its associated image.

3
00:00:12,164 --> 00:00:16,909
A model learns patterns from labelled examples and predicts one of six categories.

4
00:00:17,029 --> 00:00:21,020
It does not search the internet or independently prove whether a claim is true.

5
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:27,790
This video builds from those basics to the actual architecture, decisions, results, and failure cases.

6
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:36,847
The six labels are True, Satire or Parody, Misleading Content, Imposter Content, False Connection, and Manipulated Content.

7
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:40,510
False Connection concerns a mismatch between text and image.

8
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:43,409
Manipulated Content includes edited images.

9
00:00:43,529 --> 00:00:49,876
In Fakeddit, labels largely come from the source community rather than independent verification of every post.

10
00:00:49,996 --> 00:00:53,975
A system can therefore learn community style and dataset shortcuts.

11
00:00:54,095 --> 00:00:59,176
High benchmark accuracy does not automatically make it a reliable real world fact checker.

12
00:00:59,676 --> 00:01:08,003
We used the faculty linked Fakeddit release with three files: multimodal train, multimodal validate, and multimodal test public.

13
00:01:08,123 --> 00:01:13,311
The original files contain six hundred eighty two thousand six hundred sixty one rows.

14
00:01:13,431 --> 00:01:20,533
Exactly four hundred ninety one images failed strict decoding and were excluded with approval, before model results were used.

15
00:01:20,653 --> 00:01:22,558
The usable counts are on screen.

16
00:01:22,678 --> 00:01:28,932
Original files, identities, titles, six labels, and split assignments were preserved.

17
00:01:29,052 --> 00:01:32,015
We did not manually create replacement splits.

18
00:01:32,515 --> 00:01:34,396
Training data teaches the model.

19
00:01:34,516 --> 00:01:38,282
Validation data compares checkpoints without updating their weights.

20
00:01:38,402 --> 00:01:41,618
The held out test is reserved for the final frozen choice.

21
00:01:41,738 --> 00:01:48,678
If we repeatedly change a model after seeing its test result, that test stops being an honest final examination.

22
00:01:48,798 --> 00:01:56,104
Separate post identities do not guarantee independent images: our audit found cross split byte identical image groups.

23
00:01:56,224 --> 00:02:00,471
That is a remaining leakage and comparability risk, not something to hide.

24
00:02:00,971 --> 00:02:04,044
A computer cannot feed raw words directly into BERT.

25
00:02:04,164 --> 00:02:09,049
Its WordPiece tokenizer converts a title into integer token identifiers.

26
00:02:09,169 --> 00:02:12,486
An attention mask identifies real tokens and padding.

27
00:02:12,606 --> 00:02:16,835
We cap inputs at one hundred twenty eight tokens, then pad each batch.

28
00:02:16,955 --> 00:02:20,117
BERT uses attention to relate words to their context.

29
00:02:20,237 --> 00:02:26,155
Its special classification token produces a seven hundred sixty eight dimensional representation.

30
00:02:26,275 --> 00:02:31,637
Changing tokenization or truncation changes the experiment and must be registered and checked.

31
00:02:32,137 --> 00:02:40,203
Images are decoded, converted to red, green, and blue channels, and normalized using the expected ImageNet channel statistics.

32
00:02:40,323 --> 00:02:46,883
Training uses a reproducible random resized crop, linked to the seed, epoch, and example identity.

33
00:02:47,003 --> 00:02:54,128
Evaluation resizes the shorter side to two hundred thirty two, then center crops to two hundred twenty four pixels.

34
00:02:54,248 --> 00:02:57,427
These rules prevent arbitrary evaluation changes.

35
00:02:57,547 --> 00:03:03,192
Skipping normalization or using a different crop can shift the input distribution and change predictions.

36
00:03:03,692 --> 00:03:09,880
The text encoder is BERT base uncased, initialized from our verified selected text only checkpoint.

37
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,921
The image encoder is ResNet fifty, initialized with ImageNet version two weights.

38
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:20,366
ResNet uses residual connections to help information and gradients flow through a deep network.

39
00:03:20,486 --> 00:03:25,411
We remove its original image classifier and keep two thousand forty eight pooled features.

40
00:03:25,531 --> 00:03:28,182
Both encoders and the fusion head are fine tuned.

41
00:03:28,302 --> 00:03:34,759
Starting from useful pretrained representations reduces the burden compared with learning everything from random weights.

42
00:03:35,259 --> 00:03:38,151
The two encoders produce different sized vectors.

43
00:03:38,271 --> 00:03:42,674
Separate learned linear projections map each to five hundred twelve dimensions.

44
00:03:42,794 --> 00:03:45,863
Layer normalization helps control feature scale.

45
00:03:45,983 --> 00:03:50,985
These projections are learned adapters, not manually defined semantic coordinates.

46
00:03:51,105 --> 00:03:55,670
Without matching sizes, elementwise maximum cannot combine the vectors.

47
00:03:55,790 --> 00:04:02,461
Removing normalization or changing the projection size would be a different architecture requiring new training and validation.

48
00:04:02,581 --> 00:04:06,299
We did not run an ablation proving this size is universally optimal.

49
00:04:06,799 --> 00:04:12,132
Maximum fusion compares the two projected values at every position and keeps the larger one.

50
00:04:12,252 --> 00:04:18,406
The small example on screen illustrates the operation; the real vectors have five hundred twelve positions.

51
00:04:18,526 --> 00:04:21,745
Some positions can come from text and others from the image.

52
00:04:21,865 --> 00:04:24,556
This is not voting between class predictions.

53
00:04:24,676 --> 00:04:26,844
Addition changes the combined scale.

54
00:04:26,964 --> 00:04:30,795
Concatenation creates a larger vector and needs a compatible head.

55
00:04:30,915 --> 00:04:36,405
Those are legitimate alternatives, but comparing them requires separately controlled experiments.

56
00:04:36,905 --> 00:04:48,573
The fused vector enters a linear layer with two hundred fifty six outputs, a GELU activation, dropout with probability zero point two, and a final layer producing six scores called logits.

57
00:04:48,693 --> 00:04:50,417
GELU adds nonlinearity.

58
00:04:50,537 --> 00:04:56,578
Dropout discourages dependence on particular activations during training and is off during evaluation.

59
00:04:56,698 --> 00:05:00,403
For training, cross entropy takes the raw logits directly.

60
00:05:00,523 --> 00:05:06,214
For reporting predictions, softmax converts logits into six probabilities that sum to one.

61
00:05:06,334 --> 00:05:09,155
The largest probability gives the predicted label.

62
00:05:09,275 --> 00:05:13,444
The network has about one hundred thirty four million trainable parameters.

63
00:05:13,944 --> 00:05:19,329
Cross entropy penalizes the model when it gives too little probability to the correct training label.

64
00:05:19,449 --> 00:05:23,168
Backpropagation calculates how weights contributed to that loss.

65
00:05:23,288 --> 00:05:27,871
Adam W updates the parameters, with weight decay for regularization.

66
00:05:27,991 --> 00:05:37,442
The learning rates differ: BERT uses one times ten to the minus five, the image encoder one times ten to the minus four, and the head one times ten to the minus three.

67
00:05:37,562 --> 00:05:40,740
A warmup and decay scheduler controls their evolution.

68
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:46,908
We used one Tesla T four cloud GPU in Kaggle, even when two devices were allocated.

69
00:05:47,028 --> 00:05:49,602
Eight examples fit into each microbatch.

70
00:05:49,722 --> 00:05:56,603
Accumulating gradients over four microbatches produces an effective batch of thirty two before an optimizer boundary.

71
00:05:56,723 --> 00:06:04,413
Mixed precision saves memory, a gradient scaler helps handle numerical range, and gradient clipping limits extreme updates.

72
00:06:04,533 --> 00:06:06,958
These details affect learning and recovery.

73
00:06:07,078 --> 00:06:11,453
Changing batch size or the schedule midway would break the controlled continuation.

74
00:06:11,953 --> 00:06:14,950
One epoch is a complete pass over the training set.

75
00:06:15,070 --> 00:06:19,455
Our registered budget was four epochs, each followed by a full validation.

76
00:06:19,575 --> 00:06:26,031
The selection rule was highest validation macro F one, then lower loss, then earlier epoch if still tied.

77
00:06:26,151 --> 00:06:30,385
An early stopping rule existed, but this run completed all four epochs.

78
00:06:30,505 --> 00:06:33,756
Training interruptions were resumed within the same experiment.

79
00:06:33,876 --> 00:06:38,342
After validation selection was frozen, we ran one full final test.

80
00:06:38,462 --> 00:06:42,465
Independent metric recomputation is not an additional model test.

81
00:06:42,965 --> 00:06:48,337
Epoch three had the highest validation accuracy, eighty eight point five three four four percent.

82
00:06:48,457 --> 00:06:56,023
Epoch four had the highest macro F one, zero point eight two nine three zero three, so epoch four won under the declared rule.

83
00:06:56,143 --> 00:07:00,134
Its validation accuracy was slightly lower, and its loss was higher.

84
00:07:00,254 --> 00:07:04,496
Better class decisions can coexist with worse confidence on mistakes.

85
00:07:04,616 --> 00:07:11,387
We retained that tradeoff rather than changing the rule after seeing results or picking whichever checkpoint looked best on the test.

86
00:07:11,887 --> 00:07:19,644
Final test accuracy was eighty eight point one four five eight percent, and macro F one was zero point eight two six four one five.

87
00:07:19,764 --> 00:07:22,140
Accuracy measures the fraction correct.

88
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:26,537
Micro F one equals accuracy in this exhaustive single label task.

89
00:07:26,657 --> 00:07:30,219
Precision asks how many predicted class items were correct.

90
00:07:30,339 --> 00:07:33,643
Recall asks how many actual class items were found.

91
00:07:33,763 --> 00:07:36,279
F one balances precision and recall.

92
00:07:36,399 --> 00:07:41,222
Macro F one weights classes equally; weighted F one uses class frequency.

93
00:07:41,342 --> 00:07:44,156
Balanced accuracy averages class recalls.

94
00:07:44,276 --> 00:07:47,257
Cross entropy also evaluates confidence.

95
00:07:47,377 --> 00:07:51,008
Imposter Content remained the weakest class by test F one.

96
00:07:51,128 --> 00:07:54,889
Confusion matrices show which classes were mistaken for each other.

97
00:07:55,389 --> 00:08:00,173
The matching paper reference is six way BERT plus ResNet fifty with maximum fusion.

98
00:08:00,293 --> 00:08:07,348
Our test accuracy is two point two six five eight percentage points higher, exceeding the numerical one point aspiration.

99
00:08:07,468 --> 00:08:11,412
However, the paper used a different feature and training pipeline.

100
00:08:11,532 --> 00:08:17,659
Our available image cohort excludes decoding failures, and we used extra text warm start training.

101
00:08:17,779 --> 00:08:24,828
One seed, cross split image duplication, and the unresolved paper versus release row count difference limit the claim.

102
00:08:24,948 --> 00:08:28,315
Comparable paper F one and loss values were not reported.

103
00:08:28,815 --> 00:08:36,905
Preparation took time because image decoding, storage, transfers, and slow loading had to be resolved before trustworthy training.

104
00:08:37,025 --> 00:08:43,912
We preserved original image bytes, verified packed inputs, and used a cached archive reader on the cloud runtime.

105
00:08:44,032 --> 00:08:53,664
Recovery saved not just weights, but optimizer, scheduler, scaler, random states, sample cursor, crop rules, and selection history.

106
00:08:53,784 --> 00:08:57,171
A spinner or old notebook output is not proof of activity.

107
00:08:57,291 --> 00:09:05,582
Fresh counters, timestamps, saved outputs, checksums, and independent prediction checks establish what actually completed.

108
00:09:06,082 --> 00:09:07,326
Why these models?

109
00:09:07,446 --> 00:09:14,654
They give a recognizable paper aligned baseline, useful pretrained features, and a feasible T four implementation.

110
00:09:14,774 --> 00:09:17,435
We did not prove they beat every newer alternative.

111
00:09:17,555 --> 00:09:24,627
CLIP, vision transformers, and newer text encoders are possible future studies, not completed experiments.

112
00:09:24,747 --> 00:09:30,560
Removing images, changing fusion, or freezing an encoder requires retraining and validation.

113
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:34,165
Skipping the held out test removes our final performance check.

114
00:09:34,285 --> 00:09:40,154
Next is the image only baseline, followed by controlled ablations and repeated seeds if approved.

115
00:09:40,274 --> 00:09:45,096
You now have the pipeline, its rationale, its evidence, and its limits.
